@[init-system step]
The init system starts and stops everything else. The base system you just
installed uses @[simpleinit-msb] with SMGL's init.d scripts, Source Mage's
classic setup. If that is what you want, there is nothing to do here: type
@[next].

To use @[systemd] instead (journald, logind, unit files, systemd-boot):
  @[enchantment install init systemd]
This needs network in the live system (see @[guide network]): sorcery downloads
and compiles systemd, linux-pam, polkit and a few libraries, and recompiles the
installed spells that gain systemd or PAM support (util-linux, e2fsprogs,
shadow, openssh, ...). Expect anywhere from 20 minutes to a few hours,
depending on the machine. It also replaces simpleinit-msb and eudev, and writes
a PAM setup (/etc/pam.d/system-auth and system-login).

It runs two casts: systemd and what it needs (util-linux is rebuilt there with
sulogin), then the recompiles (util-linux again, now that systemd exists).
Each ends with the spells' notices; they are also kept in
$ENCHANT_TARGET/tmp/cast/backup/*/notice_log (in /tmp: read them before
rebooting).

systemd isn't on the ISO (to keep it small), hence the compiling. sorcery
normally uses one CPU core (its MAKE_NJOBS setting); unless you set it, this
command uses all cores while it runs and then puts the setting back. To set
it for good (the edit-confs step comes back to it; leaving 2 or 3 threads
free keeps a machine usable while it compiles): @[enchantment chroot],
@[sorcery], Option Menu, Parallel/Cached/Distributed Compiling, EXTRA_JOBS
(Adjust, then OK). If the network fails before systemd is installed, the
command puts simpleinit-msb back, so the system still boots.

Afterwards, @[todo systemd] explains networking, services and logs under
systemd, and @[todo systemd-boot] its boot loader (you can still use GRUB).

You can also switch later, from the installed system, by casting systemd; the
installer's command does the extra work (answers, PAM, recompiles) for you.

When you are ready, type @[next].
